in a summer of shark sightings racist  tweets from the White House and children  held in cages along the southern border  we may have lost sight of the distinct  possibility we could end up at war with  Iran some suggests were already engaged  in a tit-for-tat  cyber war Iran has seized a British ship  claims it disrupted a CIA spy network  and after in what the US says is a  deliberately provocative manner in the  strategic shipping lanes of the gulf of  Hormuz and while president Trump has not  authorized any military action he has  disclosed the US has conducted cyber  attacks on Iran and issued a stern  warning we'll see what happens with Iran  Iran has to be very very careful joining  us now to talk about all this is Karen  Greenberg the executive director of the  Center for national security at the  Fordham Law School and by Skype from  Cambridge Massachusetts Matt bond a  professor at Harvard one of the world's  great experts on nuclear proliferation  and terrorism thank you both for being  here Matt but let me start with you do  you think we're on the brink of  something I think it's very dangerous  right now  we have a situation in the fairly  crowded Strait of Hormuz with lots of  different military forces operating not  only there but in Iraq in Yemen in Syria  there are lots of things that could  clash inadvertently and be the spark for  the beginning of a war meanwhile there  is strong tensions because of the  sanctions that the United States has  imposed after pulling out of the Iran  nuclear deal so there's and there's no  negotiations underway to resolve the  situation so that the situation is  really fraught with danger at the moment  I think and you feel one false step  could lead to something quite disastrous  indeed we've seen situations before  where things happened that neither a  commander actually wanted to happen in  1988 a u.s. ship shot down a Iranian  civilian air  killing everyone aboard during the Cuban  Missile Crisis there were all sorts of  events that almost led to war that  neither Khrushchev nor Kennedy ordered  to happen so there's just like there's a  fog of war there's a fog of crisis that  can lead to events that nobody wanted to  have happen let me ask you there's been  talk of a cyber war already underway  with attacks from both sides at each  other yeah it's almost like a parallel  conversation that's going on in the  conflict between the United States and  Iran and what you're seeing is parallel  to the Caesar ships the shooting down of  drones that we've seen what you've seen  is a parallel war going on lots of it we  know we don't hear about what we've  heard about some of it which has to do  with us attacking Iranian intelligence  networks and Iranians targeting our  financial and other networks and some of  its theft some of it's ransomware and  some of it's just to show that they can  do it but it is more getting more and  more intense it's been going on over the  course of the last couple of months and  it has not helped by the fact that the  bellicose language from the president  makes it so that he's pushing Iran  further and further you know first he he  withdraws from the jcpoa then he  declares the IRGC  a terrorist organization it said and  then we begin to retaliate both in terms  of activity and in terms of rhetoric so  I agree with Matt we are in an extremely  dangerous situation without a real sense  of who would be in command to say this  is what should happen either in the  cyber space or in the kinetic space so  far no shots fired but president Trump  has acknowledged a cyber attack no  absolutely and and no shots have been  fired but leading national security  experts often say that the next war the  next hot war we see will be introduced  via cyber war and so how you make that  distinction what the moment becomes  where it becomes kinetic and and violent  and causes death is a matter of time met  but do you think that this cyber war  could lead to a shot  being fired absolutely and arguably  their shots being fired already in the  sense that there were these mines that  damaged several tank tankers that were  attributed to the Iranians  there was the shoot-down of the American  drone President Trump launched a  military strike on Iran which he called  off at the very last moment so I I think  we're closer than many Americans realize  to the brink there are senior people in  the administration who believe that it  would be a good thing to have military  strikes against Iran to take out parts  of their nuclear program I personally  think that would be a disastrous  potentially catastrophic move because we  could set their nuclear program back but  only for a few years and they would  likely rebuild in secret and there are  many things throughout the Middle East  that they could do in response there's a  real danger of setting the whole Middle  East even more on fire than it already  is and Karen in a war like that huge  casualties listen people have been  starting to talk about the difference  between the war with Iraq and what a war  with Iran would be and estimates of  people who live through the Iraq war in  positions of command and official  authority have said almost to a person  that the Iran war would dwarf what the  Iraq war looked like and and again the  allegations of they're tied to the  greatest terrorist organizations in the  world are just not true and the amount  of impact that Iran has been able to  have in Syria in Yemen and elsewhere has  been much less than the rhetoric  surrounding it and so I do think we are  in a dangerous situation and I think it  and there's also as Matt pointed out in  the beginning not a minor point the lack  of diplomacy that is that is has any  kind of space now I think you might have  seen that foreign minister Zarif has  offered to talk to the members of  Congress that's interesting because  there's a hunger for diplomacy it has to  happen and right now there are no  avenues for 